# ---- Secrets & credentials (NEVER commit) ----
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
# Backups of `server.cfg` are not safe to track either: the live Cfx.re
# license key ends up in them, and `.gitignore` ignores the file but not its
# neighbours. Without this rule, a single stray `cp server.cfg server.cfg.bkp`
# followed by `git add -A` would push the key to the remote.
*.bkp
secrets.lua
server.cfg
!server.cfg.example

# Generated database passwords (2026-10-11). `database/grants.sql` keeps
# CHANGE_ME placeholders on purpose; the real values live only in this file.
# Committing it would put both database accounts' passwords into git history,
# where they survive every later rotation.
database/.credentials.local
!/database/.credentials.local.example

# ---- Database ----
*.sql.gz
*.dump
/backups/
/backups/**

# ---- FiveM runtime ----
cache/

# Lua language server's local type-info cache. Key/value store
# rebuilt on every LSP start; tracking it in git means every clone
# ships a stale database that disagrees with the source.
local-database/
server-cache/
*.log
logs/
txData/
/txData/**
profile/
*.log

# ---- Node (website + Discord bot, ADR-0007) ----
node_modules/
dist/
build/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-store/

# `*.local.*` does not cover this: the bot's .env holds the only token that can
# change who is staff, so it must never be committable even by accident.
/bot/.env
# Next.js build output. Without this, `.next/` alone is tens of megabytes of
# generated code and would swamp every diff.
.next/
next-env.d.ts

# ---- Editors / OS ----
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# ---- Third-party resources (downloaded, not authored) ----
# `server.cfg.example` ensures all of these at startup. They are EXTERNAL
# dependencies: download them, do not vendor them. Tracking them would put
# someone else's repository into this one's history, tie us to their release
# cadence, and bury our own diff under theirs.
#
# `/resources/[nifty]` is OUR code and stays tracked — the bracket is literal
# gitignore syntax, so it must not be repeated here.
# One of these was already committed by accident; the follow-up commit removes
# them again. Verified with `git ls-tree -r HEAD~1 resources/`.
/resources/oxmysql/
/resources/bob74_ipl*/
/resources/mapmanager/
/resources/spawnmanager/
/resources/sessionmanager/
/resources/chat/
/resources/[map]/

# ---- Local test artefacts ----
*.sql.bak
/tmp/
*.local.*

# `*.local.*` above also matches `.credentials.local.example`, because the name
# contains `.local.`. This has to come AFTER that rule — later rules win — or the
# credentials template silently stays untracked and nobody notices.
!/database/.credentials.local.example
# REPLXX shell history from an interactive session. Not ours, not wanted, and
# it records what was typed into a terminal.
.replxx_history

# ---- Cfx.re Development Kit (FxDK) ------------------------------------------
# The FxDK (`FiveM - Cfx.re Development Kit (FxDK).lnk`) is the SERVER-DEVELOPER
# launcher: started from this directory it deploys its own stock gamemode into the
# CWD and writes a project file next to it. All of that belongs to Cfx.re, not to
# us:
#
#   .fxdk/                     launcher state, and its own fxserver/
#   fxproject.json             the generated project descriptor
#   Test/                      its scratch directory
#   resources/fxproject.json   ditto, one level down
#   resources/[system]/        Cfx's group directory for the default pack
#
# It deploys its default pack FLAT into resources/, not only under [system] — so
# these arrive as top-level folders and are Cfx's, not ours:
#
#   baseevents  basic-gamemode  map-skater  playernames
#   artifact    hardcap         webpack     yarn
#
# `artifact`, `hardcap`, `webpack` and `yarn` arrive EMPTY and make the resource
# scan warn on every boot ("does not have a resource manifest"). The four with
# content load a stock gamemode nobody asked for.
#
# `/resources/[nifty]` is OUR code and stays tracked. `mapmanager`, `spawnmanager`,
# `oxmysql`, `bob74_ipl*` and `chat` are handled by the download rules above.
#
# The brackets in `/resources/[system]/` need ESCAPING. gitignore reads `[...]` as
# a character class, so the unescaped rule matches `resources/s/`, `resources/y/`
# or `resources/m/` and NOT the directory actually called `[system]` — it fails
# silently, which is why this was verified rather than assumed. `\[` matches a
# literal bracket.
#
# None of this is in history to begin with; a `git add -A` after starting the
# FxDK swept 2092 lines of it in once. These rules make that impossible rather
# than relying on remembering to read `git status` first.
.fxdk/
/Test/
/fxproject.json
/resources/fxproject.json
/resources/\[system\]/
/resources/baseevents/
/resources/basic-gamemode/
/resources/map-skater/
/resources/playernames/
/resources/artifact/
/resources/hardcap/
/resources/webpack/
/resources/yarn/
